task:
sqlparse.split cuts a statement at semicolons inside a BEGIN ... END block when the block is not in a shape it already knows.

This input must come back as one statement, which contains both 'WITH' and 'SELECT meaning_of_life()':

WITH
FUNCTION meaning_of_life()
  RETURNS tinyint
  BEGIN
    DECLARE a tinyint DEFAULT CAST(6 as tinyint);
    DECLARE b tinyint DEFAULT CAST(7 as tinyint);
    RETURN a * b;
  END
SELECT meaning_of_life();

This input must also come back as one statement, which contains 'CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE':

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE mydataset.create_customer()
BEGIN
  DECLARE id STRING;
  SET id = GENERATE_UUID();
  INSERT INTO mydataset.customers (customer_id)
    VALUES(id);
  SELECT FORMAT("Created customer %s", id);
END;

A BEGIN that is followed directly by a semicolon starts a transaction, not a block, so it must still split. 'begin;\nupdate foo\n       set bar = 1;\ncommit;' must parse into 3 statements, and the statements joined must equal the input.

Everything else must keep working, including CREATE TASK ... AS BEGIN ... END; followed by another statement, which splits into 2.
